# GitHub notes — 12 Aug 2026

Paste-ready. Repo: `ScotDex/socketkill-v2`

---

## Commit (shipped)

```
feat(kill): granular security colour ramp

Replace three-band HS/LS/NULL classifier with the eleven-step EVE map
palette, one colour per 0.1 tier.

Classification now derives from the rounded value rather than the raw
float. Previously `s >= 0.5` on the raw float labelled a 0.4501 system
as "LS 0.5" — lowsec colour against a highsec number, with the game
agreeing with neither.

classifySecurity now returns `display`; label and colour both derive
from `tier`, so they cannot disagree. secShort reads sec.display
instead of computing toFixed(1) independently.

Wormhole, Pochven and unknown return before the ramp and are unchanged.
```

---

# Issues

---

### 🐛 `quantity_dropped` / `quantity_destroyed` may be read unguarded

**Labels:** `bug` `backend` `priority:high`

ESI omits these keys entirely rather than sending `0`. In a sampled killmail
(`75074344`), **all 44 items** were missing one of the two.

`undefined + n` → `NaN`, and a single `NaN` silently poisons a group subtotal.

**Possibly related:** `FITTING —` observed on a 145.50B Ragnarok where a fitted
value should exist.

- [ ] grep `normalizer.js` for unguarded `quantity_*` reads
- [ ] grep `priceService.js` likewise
- [ ] add `?? 0` where missing
- [ ] re-check the Ragnarok kill

---

### ✨ Capture `flag` at ingest

**Labels:** `enhancement` `backend` `capture-policy`

`flag` is present on every victim item in the ESI payload and is discarded
during normalisation.

Unlocks:
- exact physical slot position (`flag − base`)
- **exact charge pairing** — a module and its charge share a flag (confirmed on
  flags 27/28/29/33/34 of the sample). Removes the need for any value-ranking
  heuristic.
- empty-slot detection without the SDE (see next issue)
- higher-fidelity EFT export

- [ ] retain `flag` through `normalizer.js`
- [ ] bump `SCHEMA_VERSION`

---

### ✨ Derive rack capacity lower bound from flag range

**Labels:** `enhancement` `backend`

`max(flag) − base + 1` is a hard lower bound on rack capacity. Interior gaps in
an occupied range are **provably** empty slots — no dogma lookup required.

Sample (`75074344`, Rorqual) yielded 8 high / 7 mid / 3 rig / **1 low**. A
Rorqual has more than one low slot, so that pilot flew with lows empty. That is
statable today.

Depends on `flag` capture.

Ranges: low 11–18, mid 19–26, high 27–34, rig 92–98.

---

### ✨ Emit `victim.slots` from SDE dogma at ingest

**Labels:** `enhancement` `backend` `sde`

The flag range gives a lower bound; dogma gives the ceiling (trailing empties).

**Why it matters:** the Marshal was assumed to be 7 high across a full session
of prototyping, inferred from occupied flags. A real kill showed **8** — six
launchers, a Covert Jump Portal Generator, a Syndicate Cloaking Device. The
guess was wrong on the one hull used for hours of testing.

- [ ] read `hiSlots` / `medSlots` / `lowSlots` / `rigSlots` from SDE in KV
- [ ] emit `victim.slots` at normalisation (~40 bytes/killmail, no per-request
      lookup)
- [ ] bump `SCHEMA_VERSION`

**Caveat:** T3 cruiser capacity depends on fitted subsystems, so this is not a
flat hull lookup for exactly the ships that raise the question. Degrade
honestly — no capacity means no empty sockets claimed.

**Shares a call with the hull description issue.** If slot counts come from
ESI `/universe/types/{id}` rather than the SDE in KV, `description` arrives in
the same response. Decide the source first — SDE-in-KV avoids a per-ingest ESI
call, but then the description needs its own fetch.

---

### 🐛 Aggregation traps in item grouping

**Labels:** `bug` `backend` `needs-investigation`

Two structural properties of the ESI payload that break naive aggregation.
Current grouping may already be correct — needs confirming, not assuming.

1. **Same typeID under multiple flags.** In the sample, `16272` appears under
   flags 5, 133 and 155; `42829` under flag 5 and high slot 27. Keying on
   typeID alone merges a fitted charge with 19,622 units of the same item in
   the hold.
2. **Same flag+typeID emitted as multiple entries.** Sample had `(87, 2193)`
   twice and `(90, 17478)` **six times**, each quantity 1. Sum, never
   overwrite — deduping collapses six implants into one.

---

### ✨ Use `singleton` for BPC detection

**Labels:** `enhancement` `good-first-issue`

`singleton` is on every item; `2` means blueprint copy.

Currently the `bp` icon variation is guessed via an `onerror` handler after a
failed image request. `singleton` is the correct source.

---

### ✨ Slot counts in manifest group headers

**Labels:** `enhancement` `frontend`

`HIGH SLOTS 8/8`, `LOW SLOTS 1/3`. Colour the count gold when a rack is short.

Delivers the fit critique in one number, in a component that already ships.
Partially buildable off the flag lower bound; complete once `victim.slots`
lands.

Prototyped alternatives (radial wheel, per-group socket rails, single band) all
rejected — they duplicate icons that appear directly below, are silent on the
majority of kills that are fully fitted, and imply completeness they can't
verify when capacity is unknown.

---

### ✨ Hull description on kill pages

**Labels:** `enhancement` `frontend` `voice`

Surface CCP's own ship description (ESI `/universe/types/{id}` → `description`)
on the kill page. Full coverage — every published hull has one, so the slot
always fills.

**Why it works.** CCP wrote these as marketing copy. On a wreck report the
brochure voice reads as an epitaph: a Widow "designed for infiltration and
espionage behind enemy lines" against a highsec loss to an NPC. The
contradiction does all the work.

**Placement.** Not inside `.dossier-rows` — that block is a single-line
label/value readout, prose breaks the pattern, and it pushes SYSTEM/REGION/TIME
below the fold on mobile. Goes after `.dossier-rows`, before
`.dossier-actions`. Reuse the entity page's `.assess` treatment (faint,
monospace, `>` prefix) — same problem already solved there.

**Gating.** Not every hull. A Venture description is wallpaper. Gate by hull
class or value tier — capitals, black ops, T3s, faction hulls. Flavour appears
when the kill is already notable.

**Core discipline — the thing that would erode first.** CCP's text, our data,
no connective tissue. The page states intent and states outcome; the reader
assembles the story. Never write the joining clause ("…and X lost it to an
NPC"):

- Flat presentation makes no claim and therefore can't be wrong. An
  editorialising clause has to be right about circumstances, and a bad
  inference means publishing a snide line about a real player.
- The joke lands once. On every kill forever it's a site that mocks the dead.
- It's our voice pretending to be the system's. *Alien*'s corporate
  documentation never comments on what happened to the crew.

If it reads as inert after real kills, fix it with **adjacency** — put the
description nearer the detail that contradicts it — not with prose.

**Open:**
- [ ] Does the ESI layer fetch type details, or only `getTypeName`? If names
      only, this is a new call per kill. Check before assuming it's cheap.

**Shares a call with `victim.slots`.** Both want `/universe/types/{id}`:
description from the top-level field, slot counts from `dogma_attributes`. If
either lands, the other is close to free from the same response — worth
sequencing them together rather than paying for the fetch twice.

**Trap, flagged deliberately:** `dogma_attributes` is ~120 raw numeric
attributes and is tempting as institutional over-precision. As *displayed*
content it's noise needing SDE resolution. As *derived* slot counts it's the
feature. Don't confuse the two.

**Might become:** contradiction detection — hull class vs security band vs
attacker count (stealth hull in highsec; black ops killed by 79 attackers).
Genuinely clever, but it's a labelling system in disguise and carries the
accuracy problem labels carry. Ship flat first; let real kills show whether the
near-misses justify building it.

---

### ✨ Corp tenure on pilot pages

**Labels:** `enhancement` `frontend` `entity-pages`

Full EveWho-equivalent history **is** buildable:
- `/characters/{id}/corporationhistory/` → tenure windows
- `/v2/corporations/{id}/alliancehistory/` → alliance label per window
- interval intersection, no scraping

**Recommended shape** — the table is EveWho's, the derived number is ours:
- `CORP TENURE 4 DAYS` in the key/value block, gold under ~14 days
- or `12 CORPS · 5 YEARS` as one line
- full history folded below `MOST ACTIVE AREAS`, closed by default

**Placement constraint:** must not sit above `REPORT SUMMARY`. The card badge
reads `30-DAY WINDOW`; a 2014 employment record beneath it contradicts the
page's declared scope and pushes the differentiator below the fold.

**Cost:** naive render is ~25 ESI round trips. `POST /universe/names/` resolves
up to 1000 IDs in one call; alliance history is per-corp and reuses heavily
across pilots.

**Risk:** CCP has previously disabled the corp history endpoint under load,
returning empty lists. Don't hard-depend on it.

Blocked on `ENTITY_PAGES_ENABLED`.

---

### ✨ OG cards for corp / alliance / pilot

**Labels:** `enhancement` `backend` `og`

Same Satori + resvg renderer, different template.

**Core problem:** kill cards are immutable and cache forever. Entity cards are
snapshots of a rolling window, and Discord holds unfurls ~24h. `LAST SEEN 48M
AGO` that is actually 26 hours old is worse than no card.

- [ ] stamp the window on the card: `LAST 30 DAYS · AS OF 12 AUG 22:04`
- [ ] choose content that ages well — distributions and tenure survive a day,
      recency figures don't. Cut `LAST SEEN`.
- [ ] short TTL or generate-on-demand, **not** permanent R2 objects
- [ ] bot gate must precede generation — entity URL namespace is effectively
      unbounded (every character is a valid path), so crawlers would exhaust
      the Class A write budget

**Best candidate content:** the timezone histogram. Distinctive at thumbnail
size, survives caching, and nothing else in EVE tooling shows when someone
plays.

---

### 💡 Grid clustering from `victim.position`

**Labels:** `idea` `backend`

`victim.position` is metres from the system barycentre — precision far exceeds
grid-scale needs.

EVE grids are ~250km. Two kills within that distance and a few minutes apart
are almost certainly one fight. Would let `SYSTEM ACTIVITY 28 KILLS/HR`
distinguish one large brawl from eight independent gate camps — currently
indistinguishable, and it's what a reader most wants from that number.

Cheap: Euclidean distance on three floats, in memory against `hashCache`. No
new storage.

**Scope warning:** the output wants to become a battle report, which is where
killboard scope creep starts and is ledger territory once it persists. In-scope
version is one derived field — *part of a 14-kill engagement* — with no route
behind it.

**Unverified:** whether `normalizer.js` retains `position`, and whether it's
always present (optional in the ESI schema; pod and structure kills may omit).

---

### 🎨 `DAMAGE TAKEN` shares the destroyed red

**Labels:** `polish` `frontend`

`DAMAGE TAKEN` uses `--color-isk-billion`, the same token as `DESTROYED`. One
is HP, one is ISK, and they sit adjacent. Damage taken is a magnitude, not an
outcome — neutral (`--color-text-body`) would free red to mean one thing in
that block.

